Well, you know.

267
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.020
Tell me what it was because I think it's just as painful.

268
00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:38.680
It was.

269
00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:44.880
So, it was, like, real lava rocks over, they dug a ditch in the ground and then they kind

270
00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.840
of filled it with some lights with gels to give it that glow.

271
00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.080
And then on top of that, they put, like, real lava rocks and then along the side were

272
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:54.800
real flames.

273
00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.140
Oh, so it was still really warm.

274
00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.580
It was sort of warm.

275
00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.300
Like, they were very specific.

276
00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:05.340
Do not get near the edges of this fire walk thing, this coal walk.

277
00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.480
You saw your bare feet on little sharp little rocks.

278
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:08.160
Thank you.

279
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.720
I say it was like running over a bunch of Lego.

280
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:11.380
Yeah.

281
00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:12.280
Eek.

282
00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.300
Yeah, no, I get that.

283
00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:13.620
It's how that feels.

284
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.600
So, it was painful, but not because it was hot.

285
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:16.920
Yeah.

286
00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.100
Just because it was, like, digging into my feet.

287
00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:19.500
Yeah.

288
00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:20.960
And how many times did you do it?

289
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.820
I think we did about three takes.

290
00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:23.320
Okay.

291
00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:23.940
Yeah.

292
00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:24.900
So, not too bad.

293
00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:25.520
Pretty good.

294
00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:25.840
Yeah.

295
00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:26.760
And it was cold.

296
00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.060
Super, super cold.

297
00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:28.380
It was so cold.

298
00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.660
What was your guys' – like, what was your favorite episode to shoot?

299
00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:37.020
Oh, I love this question because it's not what our favorite episode is.

300
00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:37.640
Right.

301
00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:38.620
Because filming it –

302
00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:39.720
That's what I mean, right?

303
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:40.900
That's what I mean.

304
00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:41.220
Yeah.

305
00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.840
Because that's another question of, like, what is your favorite episode?

306
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.460
What's your favorite episode that you've shot?

307
00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:46.620
You've got a lot of big questions.

308
00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.820
I think I can actually sum it up into one answer.

309
00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:50.100
Okay.

310
00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:50.360
Wow.

311
00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:56.100
I'm going to say Dundee's because we were out of the office kind of for the first time,

312
00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:00.900
and we were on a location, and it had a slumber party feel to it.

313
00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:01.160
Yeah.

314
00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.980
Because we didn't really have trailers or anywhere to go,

315
00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:07.420
and we were just hanging in that fake chili.

316
00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:07.780
Yeah.

317
00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:08.240
Uh-huh.

318
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:09.100
And –

319
00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:09.380
Oh.

320
00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.060
For, like, you know, 12 hours.

321
00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:11.320
Yeah.

322
00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:12.260
We were just in that one room.

323
00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:13.060
Yeah.

324
00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:19.120
And it was just fun and funny and nonstop, and I got to be drunk.

325
00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:19.860
Yeah.

326
00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.300
Or pretend to be.

327
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.200
Steve kept improvising with Rain on that keyboard, you know?

328
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:25.520
Yeah.

329
00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.540
Like, Dwight would do different things, and we kept cracking up.

330
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.540
Well, don't give Rainn Wilson a keyboard that makes smart sounds.

331
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:32.920
No.

332
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:33.580
Unless –

333
00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.560
Or a burp noise.

334
00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:34.920
Yeah.

335
00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:35.240
No.

336
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:35.920
That was a lot.

337
00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:36.980
He was just going crazy with it.

338
00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:41.920
To the point where I think they were like, hey, Rainn, can you let Steve get through one

339
00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:43.280
without the sound effects?

340
00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:47.440
No, I totally remember because Steve – as Steve as Michael was making a speech,

341
00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.800
and Rainn kept hitting the, oh, yeah.

342
00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:50.820
Exactly.

343
00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.160
They were like, okay, you have to stop.

344
00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:53.980
Yeah.

345
00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.680
So, that's your favorite to film, and maybe one of your favorite episodes?

346
00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:57.420
Yeah.

347
00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:03.360
And here's the crazy thing, because, you know, there was a big debate on that episode because

348
00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.680
we thought it might be the last episode of The Office that ever aired.

349
00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:07.840
Because –

350
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:08.060
What?

351
00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:08.460
Yeah.

352
00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.600
For that season, we only got picked up for six episodes.

353
00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:12.020
Yeah.

354
00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:16.760
And it wasn't looking great, and so we had this big debate.

355
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.200
Can you believe that when you think about it now?

356
00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:21.760
It's weird now, but that was our life for the first two seasons.

357
00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:26.500
At the end of the six episodes, they had our names, you know, on paper that they had

358
00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.000
laminated and Velcroed to our trailer doors.

359
00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.560
And I went and I un-Velcroed my paper laminated name.

360
00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:34.680
I was like, well, that was fun.

361
00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:36.040
I'm going to take this home.

362
00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:36.350
Oh, my God.

363
00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:36.660
Yeah.

364
00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.420
After the six episodes?

365
00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:38.780
Yeah.

366
00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:39.140
Wow.

367
00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.480
This was after – yeah, this was our season two pickup.

368
00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:42.020
Yeah.

369
00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:47.400
And we thought maybe Dundee's would be our farewell, and I remember standing in the

370
00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:53.340
parking lot with Greg Daniels and saying, do we think Jim and Pam should kiss outside

371
00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.240
of the Dundee's just in case there's no more episodes of The Office?

372
00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:57.600
Ever?

373
00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:59.540
Like, should we have it as an alt?

374
00:12:59.840 --> 00:12:59.960
Yeah, yeah.

375
00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.440
And then if they cancel the show, you can put that scene in, and if they don't

376
00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.320
cancel the show, we'll save it.

377
00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.780
And Greg said, no, we're not going to give them the option.

378
00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.520
If they want to see you two kiss, they're going to have to pick up the show.

379
00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:12.760
Yes.

380
00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:14.000
We're not going to give them the footage.

381
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:14.640
That's right.

382
00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:15.420
But hold up.

383
00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:17.400
Pam does kiss Jim.

384
00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:18.380
She does kiss Jim.

385
00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:19.040
Okay.

386
00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.540
I think they don't kiss until casino night.

387
00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:22.240
Are you crazy?

388
00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:23.640
She kisses him on the roof.

389
00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:28.000
I know, but it was like a sloppy fall into you kiss.

390
00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:29.780
It wasn't like kiss.

391
00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:30.500
Okay.

392
00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:32.800
I say – I know, I know.

393
00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.260
John and I argued about this, too.

394
00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:37.920
You say Jim and Pam's first kiss is casino night?

395
00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:38.260
Yes.

396
00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.820
Well, that's their first, like, intentional –

397
00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:42.800
Smoochy smooch.

398
00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:47.420
Together, you know, consensual kiss.

399
00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:48.180
Yeah.

400
00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:48.820
Yeah.

401
00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.560
Or their first, like, passionate, really, whatever.

402
00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:57.220
And, yeah, I guess in Dundee's it's more you're drunk and you love him and he's your friend

403
00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:57.760
and you hug him.

404
00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:59.760
Like, my mouth is falling onto his mouth.

405
00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:00.860
Okay, well, you better –

406
00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:01.300
You know what I mean?

407
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:02.200
Like, that's, like –

408
00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:03.340
I don't know about this part.

409
00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:04.160
Because, I mean –

410
00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.600
The argument is getting weaker for me.

411
00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:05.880
No.

412
00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:06.920
Yeah, it is.

413
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:12.120
No, but, you know, when we talked to John about it, John was like, from Jim's perspective –

414
00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:12.860
They kissed.

415
00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:13.820
She kissed.

416
00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:16.200
He got a kiss from this girl that he's liked forever.

417
00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:16.740
Yeah.

418
00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:21.280
Well, wouldn't you – yeah, I would agree with Jim's perspective.

419
00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:22.780
No, I hear it all.

420
00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:23.480
I hear it all.

421
00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:24.560
What about you, Ange?

422
00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.120
What's your favorite episode to shoot and or to?

423
00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:28.800
Oh, man.

424
00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:29.740
This is so hard.

425
00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.440
I loved filming the Christmas episodes.

426
00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:33.620
They were so huge.

427
00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:35.880
My character always lost her mind.

428
00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:36.940
You know what I mean?

429
00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:37.100
Yeah, every time.

430
00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:40.560
Just screaming and throwing ornaments and stomping.

431
00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:41.920
And I loved all of that.

432
00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.980
So, filming Christmas was really fun.

433
00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:48.980
I loved the scene in Yankee Swap when we all sat in a circle and traded gifts.

434
00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:52.680
And I got that jazz poster, babies playing jazz instruments.

435
00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:53.880
Some people like these.

436
00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:54.760
Such a good line.

437
00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.300
Oh, my – and it hung by my desk for, like, years.

438
00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.640
I have one at home.

439
00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:01.040
I do.

440
00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:01.820
I love that.

441
00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:06.080
But I feel weird putting it up because it is babies, like, with the – you know, playing instruments.

442
00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:06.380
Yeah.

443
00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:11.540
Okay, but what my favorite episode is, is changing all the time.

444
00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:12.600
See, is what I'm saying.

445
00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:16.200
And I re-watched – oh, God.

446
00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.000
It was so funny.

447
00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.360
With my daughter, I re-watched The Deposition.

448
00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:21.320
My character is barely in it.

449
00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:21.600
Yeah.

450
00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:21.900
Like, really.

451
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.460
I don't think I have a single line in it.

452
00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:26.040
Jan in that deposition.

453
00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:30.020
And then when they get Michael's journal, and his journal is so dorky.

454
00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:31.780
But it's like, I'm dorky when I journal.

455
00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:32.980
Oh, we know, Ange.

456
00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:34.280
Oh, it's really –

457
00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:34.380
Do you journal?

458
00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:35.740
I love to journal.

459
00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:36.100
Okay, listen.

460
00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:36.740
You do?

461
00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:37.280
I do.

462
00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:38.200
Okay.

463
00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:39.640
You've made Angela so happy.

464
00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:42.920
I journal, too, but she is such a dorky journaler.

465
00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:48.240
Well, we brought in one of my journals that I wrote in in my 20s, and I was interning on

466
00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:48.560
Conan.

467
00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:53.460
I was living in New York, and I literally wrote something like, you'll never believe what

468
00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.300
happened today, but I got to run.

469
00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.160
Stay tuned.

470
00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:57.200
I'll fill you in later.

471
00:15:57.700 --> 00:15:58.120
XOXO.

472
00:15:58.260 --> 00:15:58.680
Angela.

473
00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:00.020
Right?

474
00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.080
But that's like, who am I writing to?

475
00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:03.060
I'm such a dork.

476
00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:03.700
You're writing to yourself.

477
00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:04.220
I know.

478
00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.140
Your future self.

479
00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:06.360
Oh, it's so dorky.

480
00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:06.600
It's a big deal.

481
00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:07.280
It's cute.

482
00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:10.000
Do you journal, like – what do you journal?

483
00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:11.560
Is it just like free association?

484
00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:14.640
I journal on my phone because it's easier for me.

485
00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:15.060
Oh, yeah.

486
00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:23.060
I'm not a fast writer, and I just journal anything that I think because I noticed – I've

487
00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:27.420
noticed in my life that, you know, you get excited and you tend to lie to yourself

488
00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:31.300
about your life and your situation and, like, how you feel about stuff because you really

489
00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.400
want to feel something.

490
00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:32.780
Yeah.

491
00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:36.380
And then you think, well, if I just say that's how I feel, then that's how I feel.

492
00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:40.660
I've been journaling for a while, but this year I was specifically like, you know what?

493
00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:44.940
I feel like I've done a lot of lying to myself about how I'm really feeling, so I'm just

494
00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:48.320
going to – because I'm like – because I'm worried people are going to find it or post

495
00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:53.240
it or, like, tell somebody because that's a big fear of having this kind of life.

496
00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:53.580
Yeah.

497
00:16:53.580 --> 00:17:00.420
And so I just made a decision to just write down every thought, like, with the comfort

498
00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.300
that no one's going to read it, no one's ever going to know.

499
00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:03.620
Right.

500
00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.780
I'm not going to have to worry about it.

501
00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:05.540
It's just for me.

502
00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:05.900
Right.

503
00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:10.980
So I just write freely anything that I think, which is dangerous for sure, but it feels really

504
00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:11.160
good.

505
00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:11.700
It's healthy.

506
00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:12.360
It feels a lot better.

507
00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:13.320
It makes me feel good.

508
00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:13.860
Yeah.

509
00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:15.040
I have those journals.

510
00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:25.080
I have those journals from my 20s especially where I lay it all out, and I do have a slight

511
00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:30.280
fear that, like, I'm going to pass away, and, like, my kids are going to find it, and these

512
00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.080
are not all the things that my kids need to know about me.

513
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.960
So I have an agreement with Angela.

514
00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.980
Angela, they're in a special cabinet, and I showed Angela where they were, and so if

515
00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:44.820
anything happens to me, it is Angela's job to go in and burn the journals.

516
00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:45.460
And destroy them.

517
00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:47.120
Because they destroy them.

518
00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:48.260
They're for me.

519
00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:49.040
That's what she said.

520
00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:50.760
Oh, my God, dude.

521
00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:52.480
But this is your legacy, though.

522
00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:53.800
Like, you're you.

523
00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:55.000
I know.

524
00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.580
People want your words after you pass on, dude.

525
00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.920
Do I want people to have me?

526
00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:00.700
I don't know.

527
00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:03.740
So it's mostly, like, that's for me.

528
00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.480
Like, and I like to go back and reflect.

529
00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:10.540
Like, I like to go back and be like, what was my mind at 27?

530
00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:11.680
Where was I?

531
00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:12.740
What did I care about?

532
00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:13.960
What was I interested in?

533
00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.000
What was I worried about?

534
00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.620
You know, and you see patterns over time when you review your journals.

535
00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:24.000
I see habits, habits of thinking, habits of behavior.

536
00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.760
It's really fascinating, you know?

537
00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.580
But it's so personal.

538
00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.580
I don't, I'm not sure.

539
00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:34.560
Like, in order to give me the freedom to really write it down, I need to know that Angela's

540
00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.580
going to burn them if anything happens to me.

541
00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:38.220
And I told her I will torch them.

542
00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:40.800
It's a best friend agreement.

543
00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.500
I mean, that's really sweet.

544
00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.940
So I'm glad you guys have that.

545
00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.060
You need a BFF who can torch.

546
00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:47.640
I know.

547
00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:49.060
The problem is mine are all digital.

548
00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.820
So that's an issue.

549
00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:51.800
Well, I.

550
00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:52.560
It exists forever.

551
00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:53.520
It does.

552
00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:55.420
And I've started a few years ago.

553
00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:56.600
I know I'm like a dinosaur.

554
00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:59.120
But that, that notes app on my phone.

555
00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:01.280
So I love to write comedy.

556
00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:03.220
I love to, like, study people.

557
00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.180
So I have a whole notes on my phone.

558
00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:07.020
Jenna, I don't even know if I've ever shared this with you.

559
00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:11.940
Of just observations of people, situations, things that I might find funny.

560
00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:13.980
I have a handwritten notebook of that.

561
00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:15.620
You do?

562
00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:17.520
Yeah, I wrote one down the other day.

563
00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:22.620
I said, in case I ever need to play characters, I can look at all these notes.

564
00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.220
Woman who reapplies her lipstick immediately after finishing a meal.

565
00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:27.960
That's a person.

566
00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:28.500
It is.

567
00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:29.140
That's a thing.

568
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:29.920
That's a full character.

569
00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:31.300
I have a question for both of you.

570
00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:34.340
Because I know what some of this stuff is like.

571
00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.360
What did you hate doing?

572
00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.080
Or, like, the process of making?

573
00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:44.820
By the end of the run, it was less fun to sit in the background of all the scenes.

574
00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:47.400
I think about that a lot when I'm watching.

575
00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.340
Because you guys really be doing that.

576
00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:53.020
Like, you guys are in the back of a lot of shots.

577
00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:53.600
Everybody.

578
00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:57.200
Like, all of the one-offs, like the talking heads.

579
00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:01.640
And you don't even think, when you're watching, honestly, I don't even think about it unless,

580
00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:06.360
like, I religiously watch, like, the bloopers of all the stuff.

581
00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.600
And that's when I notice, I'm like, oh my god.

582
00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:10.300
Leslie David Baker.

583
00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:10.580
Yeah.

584
00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:12.320
And Creed are in the back.

585
00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:12.600
Yeah.

586
00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.920
In the back of every single one.

587
00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:15.380
Yeah.

588
00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:16.440
I know.

589
00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:17.420
For nine years.

590
00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:18.200
For nine years.

591
00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:18.680
For nine years.

592
00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.340
I sometimes lucked out because I was behind a partition.

593
00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:23.060
And so I could be like, come on, guys.

594
00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:24.700
You can't see Angela riding here.

595
00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:25.320
Come on.

596
00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.040
You can barely see me when I stand up.

597
00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:32.420
I know, for me, the thing that really got old was the fake pregnant belly.

598
00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:33.740
Oh, I'm sure.

599
00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.600
Because you had a bodysuit with the belly.

600
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.540
And it was itchy and heavy.

601
00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:40.900
And Jenna had it firSt.

602
00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:41.340
Right.

603
00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.960
And she was really griping about it.

604
00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:44.200
And I was like, okay, all right.

605
00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.200
It can't be that bad.

606
00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:45.720
Oh, God.

607
00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.140
And then I had it.

608
00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:49.460
And I was like, are you kidding me with this thing?

609
00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:50.520
Like, to go pee?

610
00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:51.200
Forget it.

611
00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:52.120
You're mic'd under the-

612
00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.200
It was 20 minutes to get out of it.

613
00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.020
Yeah, you're under this pillow thing.

614
00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:56.360
Wow.

615
00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:57.440
So it's not just a belly.

616
00:20:57.580 --> 00:20:59.420
It's like a full piece.

617
00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:00.460
It's like a-

618
00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:01.880
It's like a wetsuit.

619
00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.320
But then a little pocket that a belly goes in.

620
00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:06.440
So your legs and your arms are in that thing.

621
00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:06.920
Yeah.

622
00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:08.780
You step into it, right?

623
00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:11.220
And how about this?

624
00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:12.300
We haven't gotten there yet.

625
00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:16.600
But when Robert California has this big party, we all go in the swimming pool.

626
00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.840
And they had me swim with that fake belly.

627
00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:20.540
Guess what?

628
00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:21.640
It absorbs water.

629
00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:24.740
I was like, oh, my God.

630
00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:26.280
I'm shocked you didn't sink to the bottom.

631
00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:27.200
With your tiny self.

632
00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:27.800
I know.

633
00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.500
I got a good core workout.

634
00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.480
Do you have a favorite season of The Office?

635
00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:33.260
I do.

636
00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:33.860
You do?

637
00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:34.440
What is it?

638
00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:34.720
Okay.

639
00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.380
Three, four, and also five.

640
00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:40.480
But three and four are like unbelievable.

641
00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:42.660
Five is also incredible.

642
00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:43.120
Yeah.

643
00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:43.720
Incredible.

644
00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:48.100
But three and four, like, it's just like Jim and Pam get together around there.

645
00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:49.200
Things are good.

646
00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:51.440
We're like, Michael's not leaving yet.

647
00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.780
We're in a good zone, man.

648
00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:53.040
Yeah.

649
00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.060
There's not conflict as much yet.

650
00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:56.940
There's not, like, the senator yet.

651
00:21:57.140 --> 00:21:57.340
Yeah.

652
00:21:57.340 --> 00:21:59.540
There's not Robert California yet.

653
00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:00.760
There's not Joe.

654
00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:01.780
There's not Gabe yet.

655
00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:02.480
I love it.

656
00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:03.680
It stays really good.

657
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:06.980
But those just couple seasons are just, like, happiness.

658
00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:08.840
Can I bring you into a controversy?

659
00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:09.760
Yeah, please.

660
00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:16.680
Do you think that it was okay that Jim bought his parents' house in Frame Tobey without telling Pam first?

661
00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:17.620
Where do you stand?

662
00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:18.760
Interesting.

663
00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:20.320
Romantic or controlling?

664
00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:21.740
I don't love it.

665
00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:28.500
But also, people in their relationships have their own what's okay and what's not.

666
00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:29.420
That's a great answer.

667
00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:32.460
Even though these are not real characters, they are in the show.

668
00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:36.600
And it's not, it's up to Pam whether it was okay or not.

669
00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:37.440
Not me.

670
00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:37.840
Exactly.

671
00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:38.240
Do you know what I mean?

672
00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.260
And I think it made Pam, like, life.

673
00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:41.840
She felt so taken care of.

674
00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:47.040
And also what I liked was that at first, you're like, you thought this without telling me?

675
00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:49.080
And, like, look at all these weird things going on.

676
00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:54.200
And you have this whole concern and everyone that's watching is like, yeah, what the hell is going on?

677
00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:59.900
And then it's this, like, very sweet, touching moment of you being like, I love it.

678
00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:00.700
Like, look at this.

679
00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.940
Like, I don't even care about any of the anything, but you bought me this, you know?

680
00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:06.020
You bought me a house.

681
00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:06.600
It's amazing.

682
00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:10.300
So it's like, I think you can see it from both sides.

683
00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:14.520
Like, I wouldn't want someone to do that in my life.

684
00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:19.260
But also, it's like, it depends on the situation and the person and your life and the thing, you know?

685
00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:23.300
And I think also what came before it was Roy, who was so noncommittal.

686
00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:24.960
They were engaged for so long.

687
00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:25.440
Roy sucks.

688
00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:26.860
And then you meet, yes.

689
00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:29.820
And then you meet Jim, who's, like, all in.

690
00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:30.240
Yeah.

691
00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:31.860
And how good that must have felt.

692
00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:34.240
Yeah, it's true, actually.

693
00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:37.380
And someone who didn't do anything for Pam ever.

694
00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:41.980
And then it's like, yeah, maybe it's so far the other direction that it could be too far.

695
00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:48.260
But it's so nothing like she's ever experienced that it's, like, really touching to do that, you know?

696
00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:49.120
Which is sweet.

697
00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:54.880
It's interesting because when that episode came up, I said, and we've had these conversations before.

698
00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:58.340
What do you do when something has been written for your character?

699
00:23:58.380 --> 00:23:59.500
Yeah, that you don't agree with.

700
00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:03.120
Yeah, that you're like, how do I get behind this?

701
00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:04.760
I don't know how to wrap my head around it.

702
00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:09.400
And Greg said something similar, which is that Jenna wouldn't like this, but Pam does.

703
00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:17.480
And very similarly, Jenna does not like being tickled, but Pam seemed to respond well to it.

704
00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:20.920
When Roy tickles her, she would always, like, come around.

705
00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:22.140
She liked being tickled.

706
00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:33.120
And I was like, but this one's really difficult for me because I have a physical, like, visceral response to tickling that just causes sort of rage.

707
00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:33.880
Oh, my God.

708
00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:35.600
Dude, my mother is over there.

709
00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:36.220
Is this true?

710
00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.000
I'm getting a thumbs up from your mom.

711
00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:44.740
I have to resist with all my might hitting the person who tickled me.

712
00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:44.890
Yes.

713
00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:45.920
Thank you.

714
00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:46.940
I feel seen.

715
00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:49.820
Tickling is a serious thing.

716
00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.000
My brother tickled me.

717
00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:55.440
We were in anthropology when I was a little kid, and my brother started tickling me.

718
00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:58.340
And I had an overreaction to make him feel bad.

719
00:24:58.400 --> 00:24:59.300
I was like, oh, my God, stop.

720
00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:06.700
And I, like, swung my whole body, and I bonked myself on the corner of a table, and gave myself a black eye.

721
00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:07.340
Oh, no.

722
00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:10.280
And I always blamed him because he was tickling me.

723
00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:11.540
Did he ever tickle you again?

724
00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:12.200
Yeah.

725
00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:12.780
Okay.

726
00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:13.460
He did.

727
00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:17.980
But he was scared, too, because I'd be like, I'll give myself a black eye, and I'll blame it on you.

728
00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:18.260
Yeah.

729
00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:20.120
Older brother gets the punishment.

730
00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:25.760
But, no, I agree that I'd be furious if somebody bought a house and didn't ask me or tell me.

731
00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:26.100
Yeah.

732
00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:26.760
Be furious.

733
00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:28.060
Jenna wouldn't like that.

734
00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:28.480
Yeah.

735
00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:33.440
But then it's also, like, funny when you sell the house without telling him.

736
00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:35.120
Absolutely bonkers.

737
00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:35.640
I know.

738
00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:36.140
That happens.

739
00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:37.640
But then it's like, you have a point.

740
00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:40.800
You're like, well, you bought it without telling me, so I thought I could sell it without telling you.

741
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:44.040
Every time I hear that line, I'm like, unbelievable.

742
00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:44.680
I know.

743
00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:45.800
Unbelievable statement.

744
00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:49.400
You bought the house without telling me, so I'm like, sell it without telling you.

745
00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:49.720
I know.

746
00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:50.660
It's a weird book.

747
00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:51.840
It's a crazy relationship.

748
00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:54.140
Maybe she had been holding on to it a little bit.

749
00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:54.740
I don't know.

750
00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:55.880
Clearly, yeah.

751
00:25:56.060 --> 00:25:56.280
Clearly.

752
00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:02.520
I mean, every week I had to, like, reconcile with a character that was very different from me.

753
00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:05.720
Like, all of my reactions, everything.

754
00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:08.440
So I can really relate to that.

755
00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:13.460
I mean, there were plenty of times where I was like, okay, can we dial Angela back just a little?

756
00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:16.160
Like, can she not always just be the bitch?

757
00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:19.420
And I loved that note that Ken Coapis gave me.

758
00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:22.920
He was like, Angela, today why don't you try being the suspicious bitch?

759
00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:29.880
And I was like, ooh, she's totally different than, like, the strict bitch, suspicious bitch.

760
00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:35.820
Was there anything in Angela's character that you did, like, resonate with at all?

761
00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:37.780
I mean, I do love animals.

762
00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:39.380
I mean, I don't know.

763
00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:40.900
I haven't ever licked my cat.

764
00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:43.360
I did really do that, Billy.

765
00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:44.640
Yeah, I was going to ask.

766
00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:45.680
I really licked it.

767
00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:46.780
I love that you did.

768
00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:48.780
I mean, they were like, it's just one take.

769
00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:49.720
We're just one take.

770
00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:50.720
We're going to be a little bit closer.

771
00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:51.940
And I was like, oh.

772
00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.120
And then I licked the cat.

773
00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:57.380
But I also like Angela.

774
00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:01.660
Once she's in, she's very loyal.

775
00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:09.340
Like, you know, like, I don't think she ever gave up on Dwight, even though they were such a weird on and off again romance.

776
00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:13.200
But he was her soup snake, you know?

777
00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:13.840
It's true.

778
00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:17.320
So I guess those two things, but pretty much nothing else.

779
00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.080
If you were an office character, which one would you be?

780
00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:21.640
Good one.

781
00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:25.740
I've always really resonated with Michael's character.

782
00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:26.340
Okay.

783
00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:28.500
Which is maybe problematic.

784
00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:37.200
I just, and I've said that before to like my mom or like my parents and my brother and stuff.

785
00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:38.880
But it's really true.

786
00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:47.220
I see a lot of my character and not some stuff like more of just like, I can't even really explain it.

787
00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:52.220
There's just something that I really, really, really relate to about his character.

788
00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:58.660
One of my favorite, favorite moments is when he can't help but shout.

789
00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.880
That's what she said, even if he's like furious.

